  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nThis Afternoon - Thursday: Dry and Seasonal\n\nIsolated showers gradually depart northeast Iowa and \nsouthwestern Wisconsin this afternoon with most areas staying \ndry. Clouds likewise depart with fog becoming increasingly \nlikely for areas that received rain last night and this morning,\nmainly along and southeast of a Charles City, IA to Wisconsin \nRapids, WI line. Much of the MOS guidance is hinted at \nvisibilities of 1/2 of a mile or less by tomorrow morning and \nthe conceptual model fits a potential dense fog scenario given \nthe new precipitation and antecedent cloudy skies during the \nday, which will allow for saturation to happen quicker this \nevening once the clouds clear.\n\nWith the lower tropospheric theta-e ridge having slid further \nsoutheast into Iowa and Illinois, mid-level ridging will take \ncontrol for tonight into Thursday. Once the fog burns off during\nthe morning, look for sunny skies and warmer temperatures \nrising back to near average. \n\n\nFriday - Early Next Week: Periodic Storms w/ a Heavy Rain \nThreat?\n\nCyclonic flow lingers across south-central Canada from Friday \ninto early next week, with the local area transitioning to \nzonal/quasi-northwest flow on the southern periphery of this \ncutoff low. Multiple perturbations eject from this low across \nthe region, starting late Friday and continuing into early next \nweek. The zonal lower tropospheric baroclinic zone looks to \nremain situated somewhere between Iowa and southern Minnesota \nduring this period, oscillating periodically with each passing \nwave of storms. The exact position of this boundary will dictate\nthe track of our storms and how the next day's storms will \nevolve. \n\nIf the boundary happens to stall over the same areas for \nmultiple days, we could see a heavy rainfall/flooding threat \nmaterialize. Thankfully, there is not an anomalously moist \nairmass in place, so any heavy rain risk will be more driven by \nmesoscale processes and rely on storms training over the same \nlocales. Even the global ensembles are not terribly excited for \nrainfall with little signal for over 1\" of rain--but \nconceptually the pattern is in place for it to occur. With an \nincreasing shear profile late in the weekend and early next \nweek, the risk for more organized and stronger convection \nlikewise increases. However, like with the heavy rain risk, any \nsevere weather risk remains tough to pin down at this time.\n\nWith the increasing risk for convection each day and weak north\nto east flow to the north of the boundary, the temperature \nforecast for the weekend and early next week has cooled modestly\nfrom what was being forecast a few days ago, with highs at or \nslightly cooler than average.",
